# THE RATCHET — the ONE declaration of every suite's case floor.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Until 0.11.7 the only floor in this repo was a COMMENT
# above the shell-suite step in .forgejo/workflows/validate.yml:
#
#   "Local macOS runs 683; a rootful ubuntu container runs 675 (8 self-skips).
#    A count BELOW those is a real regression, not an environment quirk."
#
# Measured 2026-09-03 in a rootful ubuntu container: 820 passed. The comment was
# stale by ~145 cases and nothing noticed, because nothing read it. A number a
# human maintains is not a floor; it is a number. Deleting 100 cases from
# tests/test-scripts.sh shipped green through every CI path we have.
#
# HOW IT WORKS. scripts/gate-suite.sh runs one rung, requires the rung's
# completion MARKER in the output, extracts the case count, and fails when
# observed < floor. Both halves are needed and they catch different things:
# the floor catches deletion, the marker catches a suite that exited 0 without
# running (a step whose command silently no-opped, a runner that skipped it).
# A check that never ran produces no failures, and "did anything fail?" reads
# that as success.
#
# RAISING A FLOOR. By hand, in the same commit that adds the cases. Nothing
# raises these automatically — with no auto-merge here there is no moment that
# could honestly do it, and a floor that raises itself on a CI run would climb
# to the luckiest executor and then fail every ordinary one.
#
# THE SLACK IS THE KNOWN LIMITATION, not an oversight. The gap between the
# observed count and the floor is exactly the number of cases that can be
# deleted with the gate still green, and it GROWS every time the suite grows.
# It is compounded here by the executor spread: root self-skips cases a
# non-root run executes (`id -u` gates several), so the floor must sit at or
# below the LOWEST legitimate executor count, and the spread is permanent
# slack. MEASURED on this branch, same tree, same day: root 832, non-root 840
# — a spread of 8, which is what the comment this file replaces claimed and is
# therefore still true. So FLOOR_shell sits at the root count, and on a
# non-root runner (GitHub's `ubuntu-latest`) 8 cases can be deleted with the
# gate still green. Closing that needs a counted skip() in
# tests/test-scripts.sh so the floor can be taken against passed+skipped,
# which is executor-invariant — issue #109, deliberately not done here.
#
# EVERY VALUE BELOW WAS MEASURED, not estimated. Rootful ubuntu container,
# 2026-09-03, on this change's own branch (the 820/291 counts it
# was written against were the pre-change baseline at 65d78e5):
#   python3 scripts/validate_plugin.py       -> all contracts hold
#   python3 -m unittest discover -s tests    -> Ran 315 tests / OK
#   bash    tests/test-scripts.sh            -> 832 passed, 0 failed
#   node    tests/test_workflows.mjs         -> 384 passed, 0 failed
#   node    tests/test_compress.mjs          -> 161 passed, 0 failed
#
# FLOOR_shell 898 -> 910 (PR #125 review): twelve cases for three arms
# base-gate.sh did not have — the floors.env SHAPE (a trailing comment, an
# arithmetic rebinding: both fail-OPEN on the shipped gate, sourced value 1,
# BASE_GATE_PASSED), the CHECKS registry REBOUND after the tuple (python runs
# the last binding; the extractor read the block), and the CI files' RUNG
# SET (a rung dropped from validate.yml, plus its PR-side pin, passed its own
# run and the base never read a CI file). Each arm mutated -> its cases red
# in the bash suite; controls: the real floors.env shape, a rung added, a
# forge absent at both refs. Measured rootful: 898 passed + 12 skipped = 910.
# FLOOR_shell 859 -> 862 (PR #124, two parallel rounds rebased): the hermetic
# #123B target (+1, see below) and the round-2 panel's two cases (+2: 4p
# clear-warning via the final-allow site, and the 4l setup-premise check that
# caught a cwd bug). Re-measured after the rebase, both executors: root
# 850 passed + 12 skipped = 862; non-root (su tester) 860 + 2 = 862 — the
# same total, which is what #109's counted skips buy.
# The hermetic #123B note: a regular file created in the project, not
# /etc/hosts — not a live vacuity (the pin was load-bearing on Linux and
# macOS; mutation re-run red), but it removes the EXTERNAL dependency a
# minimal container image would break.
# FLOOR_shell 858 -> 859 and FLOOR_python 333 -> 340 (PR #124 CI fix + review
# follow-up, measured 2026-09-05 on this branch, rootful container):
#   bash tests/test-scripts.sh          -> 848 passed, 0 failed, 11 skipped = 859
#   python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -> Ran 340 tests / OK
# The earlier shell +1 was the session-less case (4o); the parallel round's
# clear-warning pair is 4p — the rename the round-2 comment announced is now
# carried by the skip() and the setup echo as well, so a skipped run names
# the case it actually skipped.
# The python +7 was DRIFT, not new cases: 0.11.8 raised the floor to 333
# against a count that had already moved — the exact hand-maintenance lapse
# #109 predicts, caught by re-measuring, not by a gate.
# FLOOR_shell 857 -> 858 (#124, declined-then-taken finding): case 4n — a
# read-only .stopguard dir reads UNMARKABLE (can_mark tests writability, not
# existence). Root-skipped per #109; non-root asserts the pre-#116 stand-down.
#
# FLOOR_shell 855 -> 857 (PR #124 review): the deviations-path mark site
# (4l) and stopguard_can_mark's mkdir success path (4m) — both found by the
# review panel as unexercised; the three root-skip sites (unwritable-dir
# migration 3, holder-read 2, unreadable-ledger 1) are converted to counted
# skips, so a ROOT executor now also reports 857 as passed+skipped.
#
# FLOOR_shell 850 -> 855 (#123): the .stopguard marker's three gaps closed —
# A (stand-down spends the marker), B (reader type test: -L before -f, the
# sixth site), C (unmarkable project stands down as pre-#116, warned). Five
# cases (4i-4k), each mutation-checked red in the bash suite per #111.
#
# FLOOR_shell 832 -> 847 (#109): the floor is now taken against passed+skipped
# (executor-invariant), so it sits AT the true total — 850 measured on macOS
# (git present, not root: 0 skipped) on 2026-09-04. A git-less or root
# executor reports the same 850 as passed+skipped. The old floor carried the
# root/macOS spread as permanent slack; counted skips deleted it. The +3 over 847 are this
# change's own wrapper additions (skip-counts-toward-floor, deletion-
# under-skip-cover, old-marker-shape).
#
# FLOOR_python 315 -> 333 (0.11.8, #110/#114/#115 + two review rounds):
# measured on this branch, macOS python 3.9. The suite is executor-invariant
# (no skipIf/skipTest), so the floor sits AT the observed count, no slack.
# The other three floors are untouched and keep their 0.11.7 measurements.
#
# FLOOR_shell 862 -> 881 and FLOOR_python 340 -> 353 (#108, the trusted
# base-ref gate). Measured 2026-09-05 on feat/108-base-gate, macOS, non-root:
#   bash    tests/test-scripts.sh            -> 881 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped
#   python3 -m unittest discover -s tests    -> Ran 353 tests / OK
# The shell +19 are the `base-gate` case block (17 arms + the two
# message-asserting fail-closed cases that replaced a vacuous rc-only pair:
# deleting the base-ref guard still yields rc 2 from a downstream `git diff`
# failure, so the code alone proved nothing — measured). The python +13 are
# BaseGateTest. The base-gate case is git-guarded and self-skips as a whole
# on a git-less executor; the floor is passed+skipped (#109), so that
# executor reports the same 881.
#
# FLOOR_python 353 -> 355 (#108, the lokaal run): the two BaseGateTest cases
# pinning the job's `if:` polarity. A forge run measured the first draft
# guarding on `pull_request` — the UNTRUSTED event, which reads the workflow
# and base-gate.sh from the PR head — so the job ran in the run it was meant
# to be protected from. Measured 2026-09-05: Ran 355 tests / OK.
#
# FLOOR_shell 881 -> 897 and FLOOR_python 355 -> 357 (#108, the review round).
# Measured 2026-09-05, macOS non-root: 897 passed / 0 failed / 0 skipped, and
# Ran 357 tests / OK. The shell +16 are the bypasses a five-lens review and an
# adversarial verifier found — each reproduced against the shipped script,
# each fixed, each now a case that runs RED on the pre-fix code: a duplicate
# FLOOR key (a genuine fail-OPEN: the two-line operand made `[ -lt ]` error to
# stderr and evaluate falsy), a RENAMED enforcer file (R is not D), a tests/
# SWAP (a count is not a set), a COMMENTED-OUT registry entry (a token grep
# over raw text is satisfied by a comment), a full-content diff FAILURE read
# as "no forged marker" (grep -q on empty input exits 1 either way), plus the
# five refusal branches the header's "fails closed everywhere" claim covered
# and nothing pinned. The python +2 are the job-guard polarity anchor cases.
#
# FLOOR_shell 897 -> 898: the marker arm's exemption control (the same marker
# inside tests/ must NOT fire). Without it the exemption could widen to
# everything and the suite would stay green.
#
# FLOOR_shell 910 -> 911: the F5 inode-probe control. `stat -f '%i'` is BSD
# only; GNU stat reads `-f` as FILESYSTEM, errors on stderr, and still prints
# the filesystem table on stdout — 237 bytes captured, not empty (measured
# 2026-09-06, rootful Linux, PR #125 review). Its `Free:` counters drift
# between calls, so the inequality check passed on drift and the equality
# check passed only when nothing moved: green here, red on lokaal (task 490),
# same code. The probe is portable now and the control asserts it returns a
# NUMBER before anything trusts it — the table is not a number.
#
# Sourced by scripts/gate-suite.sh; pinned by validate_plugin.check_suite_floors.
# The validator rung has no count — its contract is the marker alone.
#
# FLOOR_shell 911 -> 934 and FLOOR_python 357 -> 367 (#107, the cap detector).
# Measured 2026-09-07 on feat/107-cap-detector, macOS, non-root:
#   bash    tests/test-scripts.sh            -> 934 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped
#   python3 -m unittest discover -s tests    -> Ran 367 tests / OK
# The shell +23 are the `cap detector (#107)` block: the trip itself, both
# halves of the (id, criterion) key, the PASS reset (including the
# FAIL,PASS,FAIL,FAIL ordering case the reset's placement turns on), the named
# report, the malformed/empty-cell skips, the absent/symlink polarity, both
# bounds announcing themselves, six hook-wiring checks through the SINGLE
# WRITER, and the clean-ledger control. The hook half is git-guarded and
# self-skips per case on a git-less executor; the floor is passed+skipped
# (#109), so that executor reports the same 934. The python +10 are CapsTest.
# Each mutation was run red before the cases were believed: the threshold
# raised out of reach (8 red), the reset deleted (1 red), the key reduced to
# the id (3 red), the hook's report disabled (3 red), the report moved BELOW
# the pending-block exit (1 red -- the placement claim, which is the whole
# reason it sits above every exit). Red in `check_caps` for the validator
# half: 7 mutations, plus the good-tree control and an arithmetic-form
# threshold control that must stay GREEN.
#
# FLOOR_shell 934 -> 938 and FLOOR_python 367 -> 372 (#107, the adversarial
# round on the same branch). Measured 2026-09-07, macOS non-root:
#   bash    tests/test-scripts.sh            -> 938 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped
#   python3 -m unittest discover -s tests    -> Ran 372 tests / OK
# The shell +4 are the WORK bound the three SIZE bounds never provided (an
# over-budget ledger truncates; the same ledger is under every size bound, so
# the budget is what stopped it; a small-ledger control that must NOT
# truncate) plus the one-definition pin. The python +5 are the four escapes a
# verifier found GREEN against the first cut -- a shadowing scan_caps
# redefinition (bash runs the last, the probe read the first), an `exit` on
# the same line as the caps test, the same in an `elif`, and a nested `if`
# inside the branch -- plus the deleted step budget. Each red in check_caps
# after the fix and green before it; the three exit shapes were additionally
# live-verified to exit 2 on a ledger with nothing else pending.
#
# FLOOR_shell 938 -> 940: the SCHEMA-coupling pair. caps.sh is the second
# reader of the 4-cell row, and its 4-cell test -- correct for a hand-edit --
# is wrong for a schema CHANGE: a 5-cell row is silently uncounted, so
# widening the row turns the detector off with every gate green (measured
# 2026-09-07: tripped=0 on two real rework rounds). The mirror is a new
# verdict word: the reset tests `= PASS`, so a MOOT row (#91) reads as a
# rework round. Neither is fixable in caps.sh -- it cannot know what the
# writer will emit next -- so the fix is the coupling row naming both parsers
# and these two cases pinning the blindness where the next schema change will
# read it. They assert the CURRENT behaviour, so they do not go red on a
# mutation; they go red when someone changes the writer and not the reader,
# which is the only moment they are for.
#
# FLOOR_shell 940 -> 943 (#127, the per-Stop cost recorded rather than
# implied). The scan re-reads the whole ledger on every Stop: measured
# 2026-09-07 at ~1.2s for 3000 rows in a realistic shape (50 keys), which the
# CAPS_MAX_STEPS budget does NOT touch -- that budget fixed the 11.1s worst
# case, not the ordinary one. Three cases pin the SHAPE of the cost, never a
# duration (a stopwatch in a suite is a flake on a loaded runner): a control
# asserting the fixture stays under CAPS_MAX_KEYS -- without it the timings
# describe PARTIAL work, which is exactly how two wrong tables got produced
# before the right one -- that a realistic 3000-row ledger is scanned WHOLE,
# and that ten times the rows TRUNCATES, so the cost is bounded rather than
# proportional. Like the schema pair above, these assert current behaviour:
# they go red when a change makes the scan far more expensive per row
# (truncation arrives earlier) or removes the bound entirely.
#
# FLOOR_shell 943 -> 947 (PR #126 review, Copilot). Two guards were wrong in
# ways their own comments denied:
#   * report_row: both stderr row-printers wrote `[ "${#row}" -gt 110 ]` under
#     a comment calling it a BYTE cap. Bash counts CHARACTERS outside the C
#     locale, so on UTF-8 the cap was 220 bytes for `e-acute`, 440 for an
#     emoji (a 100-target report: 2591 bytes under en_US.UTF-8 vs 1731 under
#     C). Its case runs the hook under UTF-8 on purpose -- a C-only test
#     passes against the broken code, which is how it shipped -- plus a
#     control that the row was emitted at all, since an empty report passes
#     any cap trivially.
#   * _caps_keys_in: the helper asserting a fixture stays under CAPS_MAX_KEYS
#     split the row into (criterion, evidence), not (id, criterion), and read
#     2 on a 3-key ledger. A control that counts the wrong thing is the
#     failure it exists to prevent. Both directions now have a control: two
#     ids sharing a criterion is 2 keys, one id with two criteria is 2 keys.
# The byte-cap pair is mutation-checked (drop `local LC_ALL=C` from report_row
# -> red in the bash suite); the two counter controls are red against the
# helper's own pre-fix field selection.
#
# FLOOR_shell 947 -> 953 (the byte-cap fix's OWN bug, found by lokaal task
# 515). Cutting at byte 110 lands mid-character whenever the width does not
# divide 110 (3-byte euro = 36.67 chars), and an invalid-UTF-8 line is not
# mangled to a UTF-8 reader -- it is INVISIBLE: grep returned rc 1 on a line
# that was right there. Strictly worse than the loose cap it replaced.
# It shipped GREEN on macOS twice over: the assertion used a plain grep, so
# the test was blinded by the defect it was testing for, and the fixture was
# 2-byte e-acute, which divides 110 evenly. Six cases now run all THREE
# widths (2/3/4 byte) and assert the PROPERTY -- the whole stderr decodes as
# UTF-8 -- plus visibility to a UTF-8 grep, rather than a grep that cannot
# fail honestly. Mutation-checked: delete the back-off loop -> red.
#
# FLOOR_shell 953 -> 956 (PR #126 review round 2, Copilot). The step budget
# charged `i` where the lookup performs `i + 1` comparisons -- it COMPARES
# element i and then breaks -- so a hit at index 0 was billed FREE. Measured:
# 0 charged against 18,999 real on a hit-only ledger, caps_truncated=0.
# THE FIXTURE HAD TO BE SOLVED FOR and two drafts were vacuous: a hit-only
# ledger trips the LINE bound first (both accountings truncate), and 100 keys
# x 3000 rows puts BOTH past the budget (153,450 vs 156,550). The case must
# sit in the window where old stays under and new crosses: k=10, rows=18500
# -> 83,250 vs 101,750 against a 100,000 budget. Mutation-checked BOTH ways --
# truncated=1 shipped, truncated=0 on the pre-fix accounting -- which is what
# the two vacuous drafts taught: the suite being green is not the same claim
# as the case having fired.
#
# FLOOR_shell 956 -> 959 (PR #126 review round 3, Copilot). The budget was
# SKIPPED on the PASS path: that branch charged its lookup and `continue`d
# past the check at the loop's tail. Measured on 100 keys + 19,000 PASS rows
# walking the table: 964,550 steps charged against a 100,000 budget -- 9x
# over, caps_truncated=0, 10.6 SECONDS. The whole DoS restored through the one
# branch that skipped the guard, invisible because every earlier case was
# FAIL-heavy. Three cases: the PASS-heavy ledger truncating, a control that
# it is under CAPS_MAX_LINES (so the STEP bound is what fired), and a control
# that the reset still clears a key after the branches were merged into one
# if/elif chain. Mutation-checked: restore the PASS `continue` -> red.
# The test stub in test_validate_plugin.py carried BOTH this and the i+1
# defect, and check_caps EXECUTES that stub -- a fixture that reproduces the
# bug cannot witness the fix.
#
# FLOOR_shell 959 -> 967 (PR #126, adversarial Codex review + Copilot round 4).
# Two bounds that did not bind, and one namespace leak:
#   * report_row sanitized the WHOLE cell before cutting to 110 bytes, and
#     sanitize_row walks one byte at a time in bash while a ledger cell has no
#     length limit. Two FAIL rows with a 20KB criterion -- written through the
#     real CLI, inside every scan bound -- made every Stop take 5.81s, OUTSIDE
#     CAPS_MAX_STEPS and ahead of the blocking gates. Slice to 128 bytes first:
#     0.53s. The case asserts the WORK via a ratio against a short-criterion
#     ledger (5x gate), NOT the output size -- the output was already bounded
#     before the fix, so an output check passes on the defect (measured: the
#     first draft of this case left the mutation fully green).
#   * a TRUNCATED scan reported its prefix counts as confirmed trips. 60 keys
#     failing, then a PASS for all 60 past the step budget -> tripped=60 where
#     the truth is 0, recurring every Stop. "Floor" was the wrong word: a floor
#     claims at least this many, a prefix cannot. Now reports nothing and says
#     UNKNOWN.
#   * scan_caps's _caps_k/_caps_c/_caps_n were plain assignments, so sourcing
#     the lib clobbered a caller's variables of the same name.
# Each mutation-checked red separately. NOTE for the next session: restore the
# file only AFTER the suite has finished reading it -- an early `cp` restore
# made two of these mutations report green, which is a harness race, not a
# vacuous case.
#
# FLOOR_shell 967 -> 968: the bounded-work case, third attempt. Draft one
# asserted the OUTPUT size, which the old code also bounded -- green on the
# defect. Draft two was a wall-clock ratio and FLAKED on GitHub's runner while
# passing locally (run 34176275062) -- the flake this suite warns about two
# cases up. It could not have worked: reading the clock costs ~291ms per
# python3 shellout, dominating the ~0.5s signal, and an instrument more
# expensive than what it measures is noise with a threshold. The shipped case
# asserts the property directly and deterministically: how many bytes reach the
# byte-walking sanitizer (20KB in -> 128 out), plus a control that a short row
# arrives WHOLE, so the bound cannot be met by mangling every row.
#
# FLOOR_shell 968 -> 974 (#126, adversarial round 2 + Copilot round 5).
#   * STDERR IS NOT A CHANNEL ON EXIT 0. A Stop hook's stderr reaches the
#     DEBUG LOG only when it exits 0 -- not the transcript, not Claude. So the
#     cap report, which must be seen precisely when nothing blocks, went to the
#     one channel that discards it, and was visible only when an unrelated gate
#     happened to block. Every existing case asserted captured stderr, so the
#     feature was fully covered and never delivered: a test that the message
#     was PRODUCED is not a test that it was DELIVERED. Four cases now assert
#     the DELIVERY -- systemMessage JSON on stdout on the allowing path, that
#     it parses, that a clean ledger emits nothing, and that the blocking path
#     still uses stderr (where exit 2 makes it the channel).
#   * the header was matched by PREFIX, so a real task named `Gate` with
#     criterion `Criterion` -- an id ops-task.sh permits -- had its rows
#     silently discarded (measured: tripped=0 on two real rework rounds).
#     Two cases: the Gate-named task trips, and the real header is still
#     skipped. ops-reverify.sh carries the same filter and is issue #128.
# The bounded-work probe also had to stub caps_say and neuter report_row's own
# echo, or it measured "sanitizer input + report line" (212 for a 100-byte row)
# while claiming to read the input alone -- its own control is what exposed it.
#
# FLOOR_shell 974 -> 977 (#126, Copilot round 6). Hitting CAPS_MAX_KEYS set
# caps_truncated and fell THROUGH, so the loop kept walking a full 100-key
# table for every remaining row -- feeding a report the truncation rule had
# already abandoned. 20,000 rows of distinct failing targets: 0.97s -> 0.14s.
# Three cases: rows READ (not wall clock -- see the flake two entries up), and
# two controls.
#
# THE NARRATIVE IS THE AUDIT TRAIL, so each bump gets its OWN entry rather than
# a re-pointed arrow. This block said `968 -> 974` above a binding of 977
# (Copilot round 7): the round-6 bump amended the round-5 heading instead of
# appending, and the one line that explains WHY the floor sits where it does
# stopped resolving. A history that has to be reconciled against `git log` is a
# history nobody consults.
#
# FLOOR_python 372 -> 375 (#126, Copilot round 7). check_caps' report-only
# window opened only when the caps-test line ENDED with `then`, so
# `if [ "$caps_tripped" -gt 0 ]` + `then` on the next line -- which bash treats
# identically -- opened no window and an `exit 2` inside it shipped green
# (measured: control CLEAN, mutation CLEAN). Same class as the `^if ` and
# `elif` escapes at 367 -> 372, one spelling further in: the window now counts
# NET `if`/`fi` depth per line, in COMMAND POSITION, and never looks for `then`.
# The +3 are the multi-line form (red in check_caps before the fix) and two
# negative controls, each verified RED against the cut it was written for:
#   * a one-line `if …; then :; fi` inside the branch must not leave the window
#     unbalanced and run it to EOF, where the hook's ordinary blocking exits
#     would be blamed on the caps branch (red against naive per-line counting).
#   * an English `if` inside a message STRING is not a keyword. A bare
#     `\b(if|fi)\b` went red on the SHIPPED hook -- the truncation message ends
#     "...if a rework cap matters here" -- and these messages are long by design
#     (#93/#94: name the targets), so prose in a guarded string is the normal
#     case. Red against that bare-word cut.
# Both mutations restored byte-identically.
#
# FLOOR_shell 977 -> 980 (#126, Codex adversarial round 3). NUL BYPASSED THE
# BYTE BUDGET. `read` DISCARDS NUL -- bash variables cannot hold one -- so the
# row loop's `${#row}` measured what SURVIVED the read, never what it CONSUMED:
# a megabyte of NUL arrived as the empty string and charged the accumulator 1
# byte. Measured on the SHIPPED hook, macOS bash 3.2: an 8 MiB ledger, 4x over
# the 2 MiB cap, scanned to EOF in 3.6s reporting truncated=0 -- a confident
# clean answer over a file the bound existed to refuse. The cost is the DELAY,
# not the report: the cap scan runs BEFORE the pending and deviation gates, so
# a corrupt or planted ledger buys seconds on every Stop. After the bounded NUL
# probe: 0.044s, truncated=1 (82x). The +3 are that case plus two controls --
# an ordinary tripped ledger of the same order still TRIPS (or the probe
# refuses everything and the detector never reports), and a UTF-8 ledger is not
# read as corrupt (the probe counts BYTES; reading characters would report
# UNKNOWN forever on any accented ledger, the false-positive direction that
# gets a gate ignored). Red on pre-fix caps.sh, controls green on both.
#
# The same round found the pin that should have caught it: check_reader_bounds
# counted `IFS= read -r -n N` and had no place for `-d ''` BETWEEN `-r` and
# `-n`, which is the NUL-probe form. All three shipped probes (caps.sh,
# partition.sh, statusline.sh) were invisible to it, so each file's floor was
# satisfied by its row loop alone and any probe could be deleted with the build
# green -- partition.sh's entry even said the probe "counts below". Regex
# widened, floors raised to 2/2/4, each deletion verified red.
#
# FLOOR_shell 980 -> 985 (#130, Task 1: base-gate's subject is the merge
# result, not the PR head). Measured 2026-09-16, rootful Linux container:
#   bash tests/test-scripts.sh -> 973 passed, 0 failed, 12 skipped = 985
# The shell +5 are the `base-gate` block's new subject cases: a PR BEHIND a
# moved base passes and names the merged tree it judged, a real weakening
# lowered ON TOP of a moved base is still refused (control), a genuinely
# CONFLICTING pr refuses (rc 2, never rc 1 — it must never read as a
# weakening), and that refusal names the conflict rather than the marker.
# (The plan text said "six new checks"; the plan's own Step 1 code block
# carries exactly five `check` calls, and 973+12=985 is what running it
# measures — the plan's count, not this floor, was off by one.)
# Two fixture bugs in the plan's verbatim Step 1/3 code were found and fixed
# here, both verified against real git output before the fix, not assumed:
#   * the "weakens" control branched from $BG_BASE, which shares the SAME
#     changed line as "moved" (`FLOOR_shell`) -- `git merge-tree` reports
#     that as a genuine CONFLICT (measured: rc 1, three-stage tree), not the
#     clean "real weakening survives the merge" case the control means to
#     assert. Rebranched from $BG_MOVED ("on top of the moved base", which
#     is what the comment already said) -- merge-tree then reports a clean
#     tree with FLOOR_shell lowered 25 -> 1, and arm 1 refuses it (rc 1).
#   * the conflict die() message read "...CONFLICTS with the base ref...",
#     capitalized, which the case's `grep -q 'conflicts with the base'`
#     (lowercase, case-sensitive) cannot match. Lowercased to match every
#     other die()/fail() message's prose convention in this file (CONFLICTS
#     in the surrounding COMMENTS is fine -- SCREAMING is reserved for the
