# FLOOR_python 397 -> 411 (#149, check_prose_invocations). Fourteen cases for
# the boundary nothing tested: prose that PRESCRIBES a command line, versus the
# command line the CLI accepts. Both sides were individually correct; the defect
# lived only in the relationship, which is why 1101 shell cases stayed green
# through the entire period the charter was wrong — every one is written AGAINST
# the CLI, so every one passes `--since`.
#
# Run red against the ACTUAL defect, three ways, each restored byte-identical
# (`shasum -c` OK):
#   templates/OPERATOR.md, --since removed   -> OPERATOR.md:109 fires
#   README.md, --since removed               -> README.md:109 fires
#   docs/maintainer/PLAYBOOK.md, `[--since <sha>]`      -> PLAYBOOK.md:565 fires
# The third is the one a presence test misses: the flag is NAMED, so it reads
# as prescribed, while the brackets mark it optional for a flag the CLI exits 2
# without — prose using the CLI's own notation to say the opposite of what the
# CLI does.
#
# Ten mutations of the CHECK ITSELF, each named per #111 with the case it drove
# red, each restored byte-identical:
#   bracketed-flag arm removed      -> test_a_bracketed_mandatory_flag_fires
#   paragraph exemption -> line     -> test_a_deliberate_negative_control_is_
#     exempt_by_paragraph (+ its leak control)
#   history exemption removed       -> test_history_files_are_exempt
#   unknown-flag arm removed        -> test_an_unknown_flag_fires
#   missing-flag arm removed        -> test_the_shipped_charter_defect_fires
#   scan floor -> 0                 -> test_a_shrinking_scan_is_itself_a_finding
#   per-form -> union of all forms  -> test_a_second_form_is_judged_on_its_own_
#     requirements (19 failures on the real tree)
#   one usage string only (`break`) -> same case (5 failures on the real tree)
#   line-anchored case arms         -> the real tree (ops-render.sh packs two
#     arms per line; `--revert` read as unknown)
#   fixture-tree escape removed     -> test_the_floor_does_not_apply_to_a_
#     fixture_tree
#
# FOUR DEFECTS IN THE CHECK were found by RUNNING it on the correct tree before
# any mutation, each of which would have condemned a correct line. That is the
# order this repo's playbook asks for and it earned its keep here: a pin that
# only ever ran against its own mutation would have shipped all four.
#   bash scripts/gate-suite.sh python -> Ran 411 tests, OK = 411
#
# FLOOR_python 411 -> 415 (#149, PR #146 review round). Four cases for two
# findings in the check's OWN work, both found by running it rather than by
# reading it:
#
#   1. A FALSE NEGATIVE the check could not see about itself. It keyed on the
#      PRESENCE of a flag to decide something had been prescribed, so a
#      FLAGLESS prescription read as "nothing to check" — and `ops-adopt.sh
#      <task-id>` exits 2 with `missing --owner` (measured against the shipped
#      CLI in a fresh ops-init.sh project). Found by probing every FORM of
#      every CLI, not only the forms this repo's prose already uses. The arm is
#      bounded three ways because a bare CLI NAME is not a command line and
#      the tree carries many of those: every form must require a flag, the
#      mention must sit in a code span, and it must carry an argument.
#      0 false positives on the real tree (test_the_real_tree_passes). The
#      population counts once written here (149 / 126 / 3) did not reproduce:
#      instrumented at 83955dc the tree gave 159 flagless mentions, 138 in a
#      code span, 12 argument-bearing — 25 and 2 among the CLIs whose every
#      form requires a flag. Dropped, not updated (review round 3): only the
#      number a test holds stays beside the code. Mutations: the arm removed -> red
#      in test_a_flagless_prescription_fires_when_no_form_is_flagless;
#      all-forms -> any-form -> same case (+5 tree failures); the code-span
#      bound removed -> 2 cases (+22 tree failures).
#
#   2. The PER-FORM selection arm was pinned only by test_the_real_tree_passes
#      — its red run depended on this repo's prose happening to contain a
#      two-form CLI. Mutating the selector to `max(key=len(f[1]))` or
#      `forms[0]` left every synthetic case green. Now isolated.
#      THE FIRST FIXTURE FOR IT WAS WRONG and passed under both mutations: its
#      two forms shared no flag, so the `flags_in & form[0]` guard suppressed
#      the report whichever form was picked. A case that passes under the
#      mutation it names is worse than no case. The working fixture reproduces
#      ops-verdict.sh's real shape — `--owner` MANDATORY in one form, OPTIONAL
#      in another — and goes red alone under both selectors.
#   bash scripts/gate-suite.sh python -> Ran 415 tests, OK = 415
#
# FLOOR_python 415 -> 423 and FLOOR_shell 1112 -> 1116 (#149/#148, PR #146
# review panel — five reviewers, six findings in this release's OWN work, each
# REPRODUCED before it was believed and each now carrying a mutation).
#
# THE CHECK CONDEMNED CORRECT PROSE (the shape it exists to prevent, in its own
# regex). `finditer` yields non-overlapping matches, so the citation's greedy
# tail swallowed the NEXT invocation whole and broke both halves at once:
#   Run ops-verdict.sh and ops-claims.sh --since <sha> --claimed "<paths>"
# — entirely correct prose — reported ops-verdict.sh for `--claimed --since`,
# flags it never took, while ops-claims.sh was never examined at all because
# the scan had already passed it. A false positive on one CLI and a false
# negative on the next, from one regex. Bounding the tail at the next CLI name
# fixed both, and immediately exposed a second: a SPAN OF BARE FILENAMES (the
# install set in REPLAY-CHARTER.md) has each entry reading as the next one's
# argument, so the flagless arm fired twice. An argument that is itself a CLI
# name means the span is an enumeration.
#
# THE NEGATIVE-CONTROL EXEMPTION SUPPRESSED ITS NEIGHBOURS. Keyed on the
# paragraph alone, it exempted EVERY invocation in that paragraph. Reproduced
# on the real tree: a genuinely broken `ops-claims.sh --claimed "x"` appended
# to REPLAY-CHARTER.md's `--ownr` teaching paragraph was reported by nothing —
# this check's own defect class, relocated into its suppression logic, where it
# is HARDER to see because the guard reports success. The paragraph now
# supplies the marker and the line must supply the subject.
#
# THE ROOT GLOBS WERE UNPINNED, and this is the one that mattered most:
# narrowing `_roots` to ["*.md", "docs/**/*.md"] — the plausible "the *.md glob
# already covers everything" edit — stops reading templates/OPERATOR.md, the
# file #149's defect shipped in, and EVERY case plus the real tree stayed
# green. The `_MIN` floor cannot catch it: it counts invocations, not which
# files produced them, and the reduced set still cleared 15. Now a module
# constant with the SELECTION asserted, not the count (check_coupling_case_refs'
# rule: a checker perfectly correct about the wrong bytes reads like a working
# one).
#
# Also pinned, each confirmed unpinned by mutation first: the no-readable-CLIs
# guard (_tool_loops shape), the docs/dev exemption, and a CLI with no
# parseable usage form (caught only by the real-tree cases THROWING, which is
# an accident rather than coverage).
#
